.TH IMUD.CONF 5 "2026-08-10" "imud 1.9.0" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
imud.conf \- configuration file for the imud IMU daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/imud/imud.conf
.br
.I ~/.config/imud/imud.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B imud.conf
is a TOML-like text file that controls all aspects of
.BR imud (8):
which sensors to use, MEKF filter parameters, output stream destinations,
magnetic declination, and logging.
.PP
.B imud
takes the path given on the command line with
.BR \-\-config ,
and otherwise searches:
.PP
.RS 4
1.
.I /etc/imud/imud.conf
.br
2.
.I ~/.config/imud/imud.conf
.RE
.PP
The two are alternatives, not a single list:
.B \-\-config
replaces the search, so a path named there that does not exist means built-in
defaults (with a warning) rather than a fall back to
.IR ~/.config/imud/imud.conf .
Without
.BR \-\-config ,
neither file existing is not an error \(em the daemon runs on defaults.
.SS Syntax
.TP
Sections
.B [section_name]
on its own line.
.TP
Key\-value pairs
.IB key \ = \ value
.TP
Comments
.B #
to end of line; inline comments are allowed.
.TP
Strings
Must be double-quoted:
.BR "driver = \(dqism330dhcx\(dq" .
Paths beginning with
.B ~/
are expanded to
.IR $HOME/ .
A value longer than the field it sets is a fatal configuration error, as is one
that fits only until
.B ~/
expands; the error names the key, the line and the maximum length. imud does not
shorten it and carry on, because a truncated socket or file path is a valid path
to the wrong place.
.TP
Integers
Decimal or hexadecimal
.RB ( 0x6B ).
A value too large for the platform's
.B int
is a fatal configuration error rather than being wrapped: it is reported with
its key and line. Keys with a meaningful range are checked against it —
TCP/UDP ports must be
.BR 1 \- 65535 ,
.B int_gpio
must be
.BR 0 \- 255
.RB ( 0
meaning the sensor has no interrupt line), and
.B i2c_addr
must be
.BR 0x00 \- 0x7F .
.TP
Booleans
.B true
or
.BR false .
.TP
Arrays
Comma-separated values in brackets; used only for
.BR rotation_euler_deg .
.SS Reload semantics
Keys marked
.B [restart]
below take effect only after a full daemon restart.
.br
Keys marked
.B [hot]
are applied live by sending
.B SIGHUP
to the daemon
.RB ( "sudo systemctl reload imud" ).
.PP
A reload re-reads the file the daemon actually loaded at startup \(em including
the
.I ~/.config/imud/imud.conf
fallback \(em and starts from the built-in defaults before applying it, exactly
as startup does.
Removing a
.B [hot]
key from the file therefore restores its default on the next reload, rather than
leaving the running value in place until a restart.
If the file has since been deleted the running configuration is kept: a deleted
config does not revert a running daemon to defaults.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS [device]
Hardware bus selection.
.B [restart]
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: config-keys device
.TP
.B i2c_bus
.RI (string,\ default:\ \(dq/dev/i2c-1\(dq)
I2C bus device node.
.TP
.B gpio_chip
.RI (string,\ default:\ \(dqgpiochip0\(dq)
gpiochip device name.
.B Run
.B gpiodetect
and use the chip it lists for the header pins:
the number is assigned by probe order and is not stable across kernels.
.B gpiochip0
is correct on Raspberry Pi 4, and on Raspberry Pi 5 with kernels from
mid-2024 onward, which renumber the RP1 controller to 0 like every other
model.
Earlier Pi 5 kernels exposed it as
.BR gpiochip4 ,
and Raspberry Pi OS keeps a
.B /dev/gpiochip4
symlink for compatibility \(em a symlink, not a second controller, so
either name reaches the same device where it exists.
.TP
.B sim_file
.RI (string,\ default:\ \(dq\(dq)
An .imucap capture for the sim driver to replay (with
.B driver
= \(dqsim\(dq in both
.B [imu]
and
.BR [mag] );
empty selects the built-in synthetic scenario.
.B imud --replay
.I FILE
is the shortcut form.
See
.BR imud (8)
and docs/capture.md.
.TP
.B sim_loop
.RI (bool,\ default:\ false)
Repeat the capture forever; sequence numbers and timestamps are rebased so
